---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { SKILLS_INSTALL_COMMAND } from '../../lib/agent-snippets';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import mcpConfig from '../../samples/mcp-config.json.txt?raw';

const toc = [
   { id: 'what', label: 'What an agent needs' },
   { id: 'skills', label: 'Install the skills' },
   { id: 'connect', label: 'Install the CLI or connect the MCP server' },
   { id: 'first', label: 'Make the first request' },
   { id: 'scan', label: 'Ask for a scan' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'Ask for a screen reader test' },
   { id: 'prompts', label: 'Prompts that work' },
   { id: 'rules', label: 'What the skills teach' },
   { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Reporting rules to give the agent' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Set up an AI agent"
   summary="<p>An AI coding agent needs two things to test with a11ied: instructions for how to test, and a way to run a11ied. This page sets up both for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or any other coding agent, then shows what to ask for.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="what">What an agent needs</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>The skills</dt>
         <dd>Instructions the agent loads when a task calls for them. They teach it how to test with a11ied: what to run first, which screen reader to use, and how to report what it found.</dd>
         <dt>A way to run a11ied</dt>
         <dd>The <code>a1</code> command in a terminal, or the MCP server, which gives the agent the same commands as tools. Install either one. The skills work with both.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Choose the MCP server for an agent that cannot run terminal commands, or when you
         want every a11ied call to appear as a tool call. Otherwise the CLI is enough.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="skills">Install the skills</h2>
      <pre><code>{SKILLS_INSTALL_COMMAND}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <a href="https://aix.a1st.dev/">aix</a> finds the coding agents on your machine and
         asks which one gets the skills. <code>--user</code> installs them for every project.
         Add <code>--target claude-code</code>, <code>--target codex</code>, or another agent
         name to skip the question. Restart the agent so it loads them.
      </p>
      <p>This installs two skills.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>a11ied</code></dt>
         <dd>For checking a component or page while you build it.</dd>
         <dt><code>a11ied-audit</code></dt>
         <dd>For a full WCAG audit with parallel workers and a report. See <a href="/guides/audit">audit a site with AI agents</a>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>To update the skills, run the install command again. It replaces them with the current ones.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="connect">Install the CLI or connect the MCP server</h2>
      <h3>The CLI</h3>
      <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
      <p>The agent runs <code>a1</code> commands in its terminal, the same ones you would type.</p>
      <h3>The MCP server</h3>
      <p>
         <abbr title="Model Context Protocol">MCP</abbr> is the standard that lets an agent
         call outside tools. Add a11ied to the MCP configuration your agent reads, then
         restart the agent. The server runs on your machine and opens no network port.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{mcpConfig.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Claude Code</dt>
         <dd>Run <code>claude mcp add a11ied -- npx -y a11ied mcp</code>.</dd>
         <dt>Cursor</dt>
         <dd>Put that configuration block in <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>.</dd>
         <dt>Other agents</dt>
         <dd>Any client that starts an MCP server over stdio works. The command is <code>npx -y a11ied mcp</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         If you installed a11ied globally, <code>"command": "a1"</code> with
         <code>"args": ["mcp"]</code> uses that copy. The
         <a href="/reference/mcp">MCP reference</a> lists every tool.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="first">Make the first request</h2>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Run the a11ied doctor check and tell me what is ready on this machine.</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>doctor</code> reports which browser a11ied will use and which screen readers
         are set up. Now you and the agent both know what it can run here.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="scan">Ask for a scan</h2>
      <p>Start your development server. Give the agent the exact URL.</p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Check http://localhost:3000/account/settings with a11ied. For each problem, give me the element, the WCAG criterion, and the fix. Then list the criteria I still have to check by hand.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent runs <code>a1 check</code>, or calls the <code>check</code> tool. The
         result has the axe violations, the criteria the page triggers, and
         <code>nextCommands</code>, the lookups to run next. For a scan alone, it uses
         <code>a1 axe</code> or the <code>run_axe</code> tool.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="sr">Ask for a screen reader test</h2>
      <p>
         A scan cannot test focus, dialogs, menus, or error messages. A screen reader session
         can.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Use the a11ied simulated screen reader on http://localhost:3000/checkout. Open the payment dialog and check three things: focus moves into the dialog, the dialog announces its name, and Escape returns focus to the button that opened it. Quote the announcements. Stop the session when you finish.</code></pre>
      <p>
         In a terminal the agent runs the <a href="/guides/screen-reader"><code>a1 sr</code>
         commands</a>. Over MCP it uses four tools.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>sr_session</code></dt>
         <dd>Starts and stops the session. For the simulated screen reader it passes <code>target: "virtual"</code>. On a machine where VoiceOver or NVDA is the default, it must also pass <code>allowVirtual: true</code>, which is the agent stating that it knows the result is a simulation.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_action</code></dt>
         <dd>One step: move, press a key, type, set a checkpoint.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_expect</code></dt>
         <dd>Checks that something was announced.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr_transcript</code></dt>
         <dd>Returns everything the screen reader said.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Tell the agent to stop the session. A VoiceOver or NVDA session keeps control of the
         screen reader until something stops it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="prompts">Prompts that work</h2>
      <p>Name the page, the behavior, and what you want back.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>Test a page</dt>
         <dd>"Use the a11ied skill to test http://localhost:3000/checkout. Run the scan first. Then test the field labels and the error messages with the screen reader. Report the two separately."</dd>
         <dt>Check a change</dt>
         <dd>"Check each route this branch changes with a11ied and report only new problems."</dd>
         <dt>Test a widget</dt>
         <dd>"Look up the ARIA combobox pattern with a11ied, then test our country picker against its keyboard table."</dd>
         <dt>Work one criterion</dt>
         <dd>"Look up WCAG 4.1.3 with a11ied, read how to test it, and test the save button on /settings."</dd>
         <dt>Narrow a bug report</dt>
         <dd>"A user says the cart total is not read out. Reproduce it with a11ied and quote the transcript."</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="rules">What the skills teach</h2>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>Look up the criterion first</dt>
         <dd>When a task names a WCAG criterion, the agent reads how to test it before it tests anything.</dd>
         <dt>Match the test to the criterion</dt>
         <dd>A scan covers part of some criteria. The rest need a screen reader, the keyboard, or judgment.</dd>
         <dt>Start simulated, finish real</dt>
         <dd>The simulated screen reader for quick runs. VoiceOver or NVDA when the question is what the real one does.</dd>
         <dt>Keep results apart</dt>
         <dd>Scan results, transcripts, and judgments are reported separately. A clean scan is never reported as meeting WCAG.</dd>
         <dt>Label simulated output</dt>
         <dd>Output from the simulated screen reader is never presented as VoiceOver or NVDA.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="mistakes">Reporting rules to give the agent</h2>
      <p>The skills write both of these rules into the agent's instructions. Without the skills, give them yourself.</p>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>"The scan passed, so the page meets WCAG"</dt>
         <dd>A scanner decides part of some criteria and none of them fully. Ask the agent to report scan results and <a href="/test-methods">hand-checked results</a> separately.</dd>
         <dt>"VoiceOver says..." from a simulated run</dt>
         <dd>Every session reports <code>targetType</code>: <code>real</code> or <code>simulated</code>. Ask the agent to include it.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         These docs are also published as plain text for AI tools.
         <a href="/llms.txt"><code>llms.txt</code></a> lists every page, and
         <a href="/llms-full.txt"><code>llms-full.txt</code></a> has all of them in one file.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
